      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A major risk with vertical scaling is that if that one powerful server fails, your entire system goes down—a single point of failure. For the business executive, this means one big, critical failure point. In contrast, horizontal scaling is much more resilient; if one of the smaller servers goes down, the others keep running, which is a huge advantage for handling unexpected traffic spikes. It's like having a whole swarm of tiny, independent workers instead of relying on one giant, fragile boss.",
      "source": "target",
