    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "can you expand a bit more on the \"unexpected traffic spikes\" part? like if we have a sudden surge in users, how does the horizontal scaling actually manage that in real-time compared to just having one big server? i want to make sure the execs understand why we're investing in the more complex horizontal setup.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When a sudden surge hits, a single, vertically scaled server can become instantly overwhelmed, like one single line of customers getting jammed up at a tiny, overworked counter. With a horizontal setup, the system is designed to automatically spin up more smaller servers—think of it as instantly opening more checkout lanes. This distributed approach allows the load to be spread across many machines, preventing any single point from becoming a bottleneck and ensuring service remains smooth even under extreme pressure. This elasticity is key to maintaining a good customer experience during peak times.",
      "source": "target",
